    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_72e27386c0ca5465d06ea5a1cbcb72cbd5eea2703e31dc0a4affbef15e430592",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "A zero-free boundary is an input interface for a prime-number-theorem contour, but its functional shape determines the optimized error scale. A classical width proportional to 1/(R log t) yields square-root-exponential decay after height optimization and allows finite improvements when R and auxiliary inputs are rechecked. Shifting into a Vinogradov-Korobov width proportional to 1/((log t)^(2/3)(log log t)^(1/3)) instead yields exp(-c(log x)^(3/5)/(log log x)^(1/5)).",
