    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_98c2411aedb962206d8ac8598b5531e95515280210f470ca36bb95cddf5e0269",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "In the earlier argument, argument variation and Littlewood's lemma convert a critical-line zero problem into a shifted mean square of a mollified differential combination; the mean-value theorem then turns that norm into a quadratic functional whose relaxed minimizer satisfies an Euler-Lagrange boundary problem and is returned to the admissible polynomial class by approximation. The later three-piece construction preserves the mollified-mean-square objective but expands its bookkeeping to three diagonal and three doubled cross terms, compresses them to c, and maps c to a reported lower bound for a critical-line proportion.",
